1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,096
Why should one person never switch a merchant on alone?

2
00:00:03,146 --> 00:00:08,496
Picture one bank admin, rushed or bribed, who switches on a shop nobody else looked at.

3
00:00:08,546 --> 00:00:13,512
By the time anyone notices, that shop has taken a week of payments with stolen cards.

4
00:00:13,562 --> 00:00:18,720
Some company cheques need two signatures, so one person alone cannot spend the money.

5
00:00:18,770 --> 00:00:21,312
Switching a merchant on works the same way.

6
00:00:21,362 --> 00:00:25,896
This is four eyes: two people look at every change that matters.

7
00:00:25,946 --> 00:00:27,768
Here is how it works.

8
00:00:27,818 --> 00:00:31,656
There is no button that lets one admin switch a merchant on.

9
00:00:31,706 --> 00:00:36,048
Instead, one admin, the maker, opens a request.

10
00:00:36,098 --> 00:00:38,256
The maker is the person who asks.

11
00:00:38,306 --> 00:00:40,128
Nothing changes yet.

12
00:00:40,178 --> 00:00:42,936
The merchant stays exactly where it was.

13
00:00:42,986 --> 00:00:46,536
A different admin, the checker, decides the request.

14
00:00:46,586 --> 00:00:50,160
The checker is the person who agrees, or refuses.

15
00:00:50,210 --> 00:00:53,880
Only when the checker approves does the merchant become active.

16
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:56,424
The maker can never approve their own request.

17
00:00:56,474 --> 00:01:01,464
The screen does not offer it, and the server refuses it anyway, whatever the screen does.

18
00:01:01,514 --> 00:01:04,968
A request that nobody decides in time expires.

19
00:01:05,018 --> 00:01:10,560
So a forgotten request cannot be approved months later, when nobody remembers why it was opened.

20
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:15,552
On approval, the documents are checked again, in case something changed meanwhile.

21
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:21,192
And the history names both people: the checker who approved it, and the maker who asked.

22
00:01:21,242 --> 00:01:23,472
This is not a rule for merchants only.

23
00:01:23,522 --> 00:01:25,440
It is how the whole product works.

24
00:01:25,490 --> 00:01:29,184
Anything that moves money or switches something on needs two people.

25
00:01:29,234 --> 00:01:31,704
A new fee rule needs a second admin.

26
00:01:31,754 --> 00:01:36,384
So does a longer refund window, and so does a refund that bank staff start.

27
00:01:36,434 --> 00:01:39,528
One person proposes; another person decides.

28
00:01:39,578 --> 00:01:45,720
Every one of them uses the same screen, the same grey buttons for the maker, and the same history.

29
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:47,592
Now watch the product.

30
00:01:47,642 --> 00:01:51,432
The viewer is the first admin, on the approved merchant's page.

31
00:01:51,482 --> 00:01:58,104
Under Actions there is "Request activation", with a short line: ask a second admin to switch the merchant on.

32
00:01:58,154 --> 00:01:59,976
One click.

33
00:02:00,026 --> 00:02:02,712
The page says "Activation requested.

34
00:02:02,762 --> 00:02:08,256
Another admin has to approve it in Approvals." Now the same admin opens Approvals.

35
00:02:08,306 --> 00:02:12,144
The request is there, with who requested it and when it expires.

36
00:02:12,194 --> 00:02:14,496
But the Approve and Reject buttons are grey.

37
00:02:14,546 --> 00:02:18,528
Hovering over them shows the message: "You made this request.

38
00:02:18,578 --> 00:02:23,016
Another admin has to decide it." The screen will not let the maker decide.

39
00:02:23,066 --> 00:02:26,856
Next, a second admin signs in and opens Approvals.

40
00:02:26,906 --> 00:02:30,600
The same request appears, and for this person the buttons are live.

41
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:32,472
They click Approve.

42
00:02:32,522 --> 00:02:34,944
The page says the company is now active.

43
00:02:34,994 --> 00:02:38,520
Back on the merchant's page, the history shows a new line.

44
00:02:38,570 --> 00:02:44,160
It names the second admin as the one who made the move, and the reason says who requested it.

45
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:53,232
Along the top of Approvals, the viewer sees the other kinds of request waiting for a second person: fee changes, refund windows and refunds.

46
00:02:53,282 --> 00:02:55,104
What does this protect?

47
00:02:55,154 --> 00:03:00,984
With one person, a single mistake or a single bribe is enough to let a bad shop take money.

48
00:03:01,034 --> 00:03:02,856
With two, it is not.

49
00:03:02,906 --> 00:03:05,856
Four eyes protects buyers from shops nobody checked.

50
00:03:05,906 --> 00:03:08,688
It protects the bank from one person's error.

51
00:03:08,738 --> 00:03:16,200
And it protects the admins themselves, because nobody can be pressured into acting alone, and the history shows both names.

52
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:22,704
So, like a cheque with two signatures: two people, every time money moves or something switches on.

53
00:03:22,754 --> 00:03:25,488
Next: how a merchant's server logs in.
